from editorial_authoring_002_184 import append
append(r'''
[111]
@City decisions|Funding and maintaining everyday infrastructure|intermediate
Who pays to maintain a neighborhood street after its construction budget is spent?|Trace capital grants, operating budgets, utilities, repairs, user fees and responsibility in one hypothetical street lifecycle; distinguish a maintenance backlog from the initial cost estimate.
[112]
@Property institutions|Disclosure and property information|advanced
What must a seller disclose about a property and what still requires the buyer's own investigation?|Compare an actual dated jurisdiction's disclosure form, material defects, inspection limits and remedies as research targets; distinguish a legal disclosure duty from an advertisement or personal legal advice.|8
Who can see the personal information attached to a property transaction?|Map land-register access, sale records, agent platforms, mortgage documents, identity checks and privacy limits in one jurisdiction; separate publicly searchable facts from protected personal records.|9
[113]
@Energy transitions|Prices, emissions and material constraints|advanced
Why can a fall in wholesale electricity prices reach household bills slowly or unevenly?|Trace hedging, contracts, network charges, levies, billing periods and tariff design in one specified market; distinguish generation prices from the complete retail bill.|6
What belongs inside a lifecycle-emissions comparison of two electricity-generation options?|Define construction, fuel extraction, transport, operation, maintenance, decommissioning and generated-output assumptions; distinguish operational emissions from the chosen assessment boundary.|7
How can a shortage of one industrial material delay an electricity project without limiting the energy resource itself?|Compare material intensity, alternative designs, refining, equipment lead times, reuse and supplier concentration for a bounded turbine, grid or generator case; exclude a commodity investment forecast.|8
[114]
@Watersheds|Water balance and shared aquifers|intermediate
How can a catchment receive the same rainfall but send different amounts of water into its river?|Trace soil storage, infiltration, land cover, antecedent moisture and timing; close an illustrative catchment water balance rather than treating rainfall totals as river flow.|2
How do researchers measure water returning to the atmosphere from a crop field?|Compare lysimeters, flux observations, weather-based models and remote estimates; define spatial scale, crop stage and uncertainty without prescribing irrigation amounts.|3
@Water governance|Aquifers across political boundaries|advanced
How can neighboring regions share an aquifer whose underground connections are uncertain?|Compare pumping, recharge, monitoring, flow models, legal arrangements and unequal access in a bounded groundwater system; distinguish mapped political borders from uncertain hydrological boundaries.|8
[115]
@Forest change|Canopy openings and successional pathways|intermediate
What happens when a single large tree opens a gap in a mature forest canopy?|Trace altered light, temperature, seedlings, competition, deadwood and repeated observation through one forest type; distinguish gap regeneration from wholesale clearing.|1
@Forest stewardship|Household livelihoods and forest access|advanced
How does a forest-dependent household combine cash income, food and seasonal resource use?|Map a consented household's calendar, collection rights, market intermediaries, unpaid labor and alternatives; do not infer a whole community's dependence from one account.|6
[116]
@Mountain processes|Avalanche maps as uncertain evidence|advanced
What can an avalanche-terrain map represent and what cannot be inferred from it about today's snow?|Compare terrain classification, historical runout, observation coverage, snow conditions, scale and update dates; keep the explanation conceptual and exclude route-selection or rescue instructions.|5
@Mountain lives|Seasonal pastoral movement at elevation|intermediate
How do mountain herders coordinate seasonal pasture, water and access across elevation zones?|Trace a named community's seasonal calendar, herd needs, routes, land rights and changing services; distinguish ecological mobility from an assumption of unplanned wandering.|7
[117]
@Adventure practice|Standards, grading and weather evidence|intermediate
What does a climbing-equipment certification test and what remains outside its guarantee?|Compare a selected dated standard's scope, test conditions, markings, compatibility and inspection boundaries; do not certify gear, teach rigging or infer safety from a logo.|1
Why can a whitewater difficulty grade change with flow and local grading conventions?|Compare published classification criteria, water level, obstacles, remoteness and consequences conceptually; distinguish a grade from permission or instruction to run a rapid.|2
How do paragliding participants compare a forecast with actual conditions before making a go-or-no-go decision?|Analyze observations, forecast uncertainty, terrain effects, training limits and documented decision protocols without giving launch thresholds or flight procedures.|3
@Adventure ethics|Training evidence and historical visibility|advanced
What would a training-and-recovery study need to show before its findings apply to a remote endurance expedition?|Compare participants, activity, load measures, recovery outcomes, environment and adverse events; distinguish research interpretation from a personal training or medical plan.|4
How do expedition archives make some women adventurers visible while overlooking others?|Compare permits, club membership, press, diaries, sponsorship and support work in a defined period; separate documented participation from later publicity.|8
[118]
@Research communities|Named experiments and missing results|intermediate
What did C. V. Raman's experiment measure before the result became a named effect?|Trace the actual apparatus, light source, observations, competing explanations and confirmation through a bounded source dossier; distinguish the measurement from later applications.|2
How did Chien-Shiung Wu's experimental design test a proposed symmetry rather than merely illustrate it?|Define the question, apparatus roles, expected contrasts, collaborators and measurement limits through named historical records; avoid invented dialogue or a lone-genius narrative.|3
@Scientific legacy|Replication, negative results and claims of priority|advanced
What can an unpublished negative result reveal about the path to a celebrated discovery?|Compare notebooks, failed replications, sample choices, later reports and archive survival in one selected research program; distinguish absence of an effect from absence of a record.|6
When can a patent priority date answer a different question from scientific discovery credit?|Compare legal claims, experimental records, publication, independent contribution and the relevant jurisdiction's dated rules; give no legal conclusion about ownership.|7
[119]
@Mathematical tools|Models, optimization and network structure|intermediate
How does a differential equation express assumptions about the growth of a population?|Compare exponential and resource-limited models, parameters, initial conditions, units and data mismatch; keep biological conclusions bounded by the model.|4
Why can the shortest route for one vehicle fail to be the best routing plan for a whole fleet?|Model vertices, edges, capacities, time windows and objectives in a small graph; separate a shortest-path problem from constrained multi-vehicle optimization.|6
How does adding a constraint change an optimization problem's feasible solutions?|Use a labeled production or scheduling example to compare objective, feasible region, binding constraints and sensitivity; distinguish mathematical optimum from a complete real-world decision.|5
@Advanced connections|Notation as an enabling mathematical tool|advanced
How can a change in notation make an existing mathematical idea easier to extend?|Compare a bounded before-and-after example in algebra, calculus or vectors; separate a new representation from a claim of discovering the underlying relationship.|8
[120]
@Reasoning foundations|Forms of inference|foundation
How do deduction, induction and inference to the best explanation support different kinds of conclusions?|Use one shared fictional observation set to compare entailment, generalization and competing explanations; make the uncertainty of non-deductive conclusions explicit.|3
''')
